## Grant tiers

Grants are expected to be awarded as a lump sum contribution towards travel, accommodation or other expenses, together with a full conference ticket.

| Tier  | Applicants from | Grant (max) |
| :---: | --------------- | :---------: |
| **1** | TBC             | TBC         |
| **2** | TBC             | TBC         |
| **3** | TBC             | TBC         |

The size and number of grants may be adjusted at the discretion of the Conference Committee, depending on the funding raised and the applications received.

- You may only apply for one grant tier. Applying for more than one grant will lead to exclusion.
- Please apply for the smallest grant that would enable you to attend.

## Who should apply

The aim of the programme is to make FOSS4G more accessible to community members, with a particular focus on people from groups that are usually underrepresented. This includes, but is not limited to:

- People who would not be able to attend without funding assistance.
- People from low and middle income countries.
- People attending FOSS4G for the first time.
- Women, minority genders and other underrepresented groups.
- Refugees and displaced people.
- Students, including PhD students, and community workers without workplace or government funding.
- People presenting a talk or workshop at FOSS4G 2027.
- People who contribute to OSGeo projects and open communities, such as open source software or OpenStreetMap.

## Selection criteria

Previous FOSS4G conferences have ranked applications using a points system. The criteria for FOSS4G 2027 are TBC, but are expected to be similar to the following:

| Criteria                                                         | Points           |
| ---------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------- |
| Nationality (World Bank income group: low / lower-middle / upper-middle / high) | 1.5 / 1 / 0.5 / 0 |
| Country of residence (World Bank income group: low / lower-middle / upper-middle / high) | 1.5 / 1 / 0.5 / 0 |
| Refugee or displaced person                                      | 1                |
| Women and other underrepresented groups                          | 1                |
| Student (including PhD students)                                 | 1                |
| Accepted talk or workshop (one presenting author only)           | 3                |
| Submitted talk or workshop that was not selected                 | 1                |
| Past contributions to OSGeo projects or community                | 3                |
| Demonstrated usage and experience with OSGeo software            | 1                |

A small number of places may be reserved for unique and outstanding applications.

## Conditions

- You must be able to cover the direct costs of your travel to Bristol upfront.
- You are responsible for holding a valid visa for entry to the United Kingdom, if required.
- Successful applicants receive a conference pass, and expenses are reimbursed up to the grant limit after checking in at the registration desk. Details of the reimbursement method are TBC.
- Advance payment, or direct payment of specific travel costs, may be possible in cases of demonstrated need. Please get in touch if paying upfront would prevent you from attending.
- Grant recipients may be asked to volunteer during the conference (TBC). See [Volunteering](/attend/volunteering) for more information.
- Unsuccessful applicants may be offered a place on a standby list in case grants become available.

## How to apply

The application form will be published here once the programme opens (TBC).

Applicant data will be kept confidential and only used for assessing TGP applications. Please make sure the email address you provide is regularly monitored, as the selection committee may contact you for clarification.

If you have any questions about the Travel Grant Programme, please email the [TGP Committee](mailto:tgp@foss4g.org).

